# The sum of an array of numbers of one limb written through Kairn (milestone2.md, "sum, dot,
# axpy, convolution"): s <- add_1_1(s, x) from 0, the lanes combined by add_1_1. If the flag is
# true, the result is a number of the format and, with eps = u / (1 + u),
#   |r - sum x_i| <= ((1 + eps)^(n-1) - 1) sum |x_i|          in both orders, for every width;
# the flag is false, and the result a NaN, when the final state is not finite. Proved in the real
# model for the steps of SummationKernels.v (sum_1_seq, sum_1_lanes in closed form, fold_seq_sum
# and fold_lanes_sum with add_1_1_adds and add_1_1_zero in the form with the power).
fold:          sum_1
arrays:        stored x
state:         s
step:          s = add_1_1(s, x)
combine:       s = add_1_1(s, s)
orders:        sequential, lanes
writes:        none
output:        r
output:        mask flag
finish:        (r, flag) = fold_finish_1(s)
value:         x
ensures:       fold_error r eps=u/(1+u) rel=n-1
ensures:       finite_or_nan r flag=flag
min_precision: 1
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
source:        Higham's bound for summation over any tree, with the bound of one rounding
license:       bound: published; proof: MIT
